## Your Task

Identify the use case's externally meaningful functions first, then assign the
requirements governing them. Add a separate safeguard responsibility only when
the use-case or typed factual context explicitly establishes that safeguard as a
distinct function with its own decision or output; otherwise retain it as a
design recommendation on the established function. For each responsibility:
1. Assign a RESP-ID and describe it (solution-neutral).
2. Add responsibility_constraints (RC-X-Y) from the constraint requirements in its group.
3. Add `security_constraint_refs` with every exact SC-* source constraint implemented by the responsibility. Include the field on every responsibility, using `[]` only when no supplied security constraint belongs to that responsibility.
4. Derive process model parts (PM-X-Y) — what information the controller needs to make correct decisions. Give each its decision-relevant `values` and the exact evidence `evidence_refs` it is read from.

Security requirements do not exhaust the control structure. Also inspect the
supplied use-case purpose and explicit operation descriptions and preserve their
externally meaningful functions as separate functional responsibilities when
they have a control decision or output. Keep those responsibilities
solution-neutral and target-blind; do not invent functions, capabilities, APIs,
tools, parameters, or targets. For each functional responsibility, include
every exact existing `SC-*` whose constraint governs its outcome. Reuse the same
`SC-*` on functional and safeguard responsibilities when applicable; use `[]`
only when no supplied security constraint genuinely governs the function. Never
invent an `SC-*` or an unstated requirement.

Leave feedback_source null on PM parts — sources will be assigned in the next step.

Return exactly one top-level `responsibilities` list. Do not create alternate or suffixed responsibility collections, and do not include control actions, feedback channels, or controlled processes in this response.
